# Frosthollow (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): mining and snow, a high valley in the Whitefangs. Short, dry, dark
# humour; everything is rock, seam, lamp and cold. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar frosthollow
greet = Warm hearth | Hearth to you
greet.broad = 'Arth | 'Arth and lamp
farewell = Keep your lamp lit | Lamp lit
agree = Sound as rock | Aye, sound
refuse = Dead seam, that | Not a chance
surprise = Well, strike me | Cold iron
annoyed = Slag and ashes | Cave it all
very = deep
good = sound | solid
bad = cracked | dead
worthless = dead rock | slag
luck = a good seam
child = whelp
pet!! = old stone | lamp of mine
money = brass
cold = cold | biting | iron-cold
tired = spent | done in
nonsense = slag | fool's gold
tag = , eh
tag.broad = , eh, eh
oath! = Black seam | Cold iron | Lamp and pick
home = my own hearth
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = stone | cousin
address[other.stage=young] = whelp | young 'un
bed = my bunk | the straw
dear! = my lamp | my stone | hearth-heart
drunk = lamp-lit | well oiled
hot = close as a forge | stifling
hungry = hollow | empty as a worked seam
hurt = cracked | bruised
little = a chip | a splinter
rain = sleet | wet
robbery = That's claim-jumping, that is | Picking my seam, they are
saying! = Rock's rock | Dig, and don't look up | The seam goes where it goes
thanks = Lamp's thanks | Thanks, stone
work = the seam | the shift | graft
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = brass | the shift's brass
boss = the gaffer | the foreman | the old stone
busy = run off my feet | deep in it
slow = dead as a worked seam | quiet
takings = the day's brass
cheated = short-weighed | sold fool's gold
mum! = Mam | Mother
dad! = Da | Father
gran = Nan | Old Mam
grandad = Grandda | Old Da
supper = supper | the pot
toffs = the city lamps | the soft-pawed lot
skint = out of brass | dug out
posh = soft-pawed | polished
busybody = pit-gossip | rattler
fool = you lump of slag | you daft stone
daft = cracked | seam-struck
toast = Lamp and luck | Deep seams | Warm hearths
drink = a jar | a pot of dark
snow = snow | the white | drift
wind = the Whitefang wind | a blow
ale = dark ale | pit-ale
watch = the Watch | the lamp-men
thief = claim-jumper | thief
bandits = pass-robbers | road wolves
grave = the cold ground | under the stone
grub = snap | grub
snug = snug as a lamp-room | warm
poorly = chesty | poorly
chill = the pit-cough | a chill
remedy = goose grease | a hot stone in flannel | pine-tar
olddays = the deep years | the old seams
blessing = Lamp keep you | Keep your lamp lit | Warm hearth to you
bless = Lamp keep you | Hearth keep you
far = a long climb | down the valley and over
stranger = valley-stranger | lowlander
